WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.526
Welcome to CogniStream.

00:00:02.527 --> 00:00:07.978
In this short lesson, we will build an easy mental picture of a neural network.

00:00:07.979 --> 00:00:13.855
A neural network learns a relationship between inputs and outputs from examples.

00:00:13.856 --> 00:00:20.292
Imagine that our input is the number of hours a student studied, and our output is a test score.

00:00:20.293 --> 00:00:24.573
We show the network many examples so it can learn a pattern.

00:00:24.574 --> 00:00:29.560
Later, it uses that pattern to estimate a score for a new input.

00:00:29.561 --> 00:00:34.029
Remember, a prediction is an estimate, not a guarantee.

00:00:35.517 --> 00:00:42.450
A network has three main parts: an input layer, one or more hidden layers, and an output layer.

00:00:42.451 --> 00:00:46.240
The input layer receives the values we provide.

00:00:46.241 --> 00:00:50.349
Hidden layers transform these values step by step.

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:53.620
The output layer returns the prediction.

00:00:53.621 --> 00:01:00.868
In the study-hours example, the input could be four hours, and the output could be an estimated score.

00:01:00.869 --> 00:01:05.070
The connections between layers contain adjustable numbers.

00:01:05.071 --> 00:01:11.427
During training, those numbers are changed so the network can produce more useful predictions.

00:01:12.889 --> 00:01:15.407
Let us look at one neuron.

00:01:15.408 --> 00:01:19.623
A weight controls how strongly an input influences it.

00:01:19.624 --> 00:01:26.857
For a simple neuron with one input, we first multiply the input by its weight, then add a bias.

00:01:26.858 --> 00:01:31.830
Suppose the input is three, the weight is two, and the bias is one.

00:01:31.831 --> 00:01:37.070
The combined value is two times three plus one, which equals seven.

00:01:37.071 --> 00:01:40.793
The neuron then applies an activation function.

00:01:40.794 --> 00:01:48.213
For example, the ReLU activation keeps positive values and changes negative values to zero.

00:01:48.214 --> 00:01:51.964
So ReLU applied to seven is still seven.

00:01:53.429 --> 00:01:57.330
How does the network know whether its prediction was useful?

00:01:57.331 --> 00:01:59.711
We use a loss function.

00:01:59.712 --> 00:02:04.604
Loss measures the difference between the prediction and the expected answer.

00:02:04.605 --> 00:02:07.437
One example is squared error.

00:02:07.438 --> 00:02:14.099
If the network predicts a score of seventy, but the true score is eighty, the difference is minus ten.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:17.450
Squaring minus ten gives one hundred.

00:02:17.451 --> 00:02:20.761
That is the squared error for this example.

00:02:20.762 --> 00:02:25.602
When the prediction exactly matches the target, this loss is zero.

00:02:25.603 --> 00:02:30.709
During training, we aim to reduce the loss across many examples.

00:02:32.179 --> 00:02:36.852
Once we have calculated the loss, we need to improve the network.

00:02:36.853 --> 00:02:43.487
Backpropagation calculates gradients, which tell us how changes in each weight affect the loss.

00:02:43.488 --> 00:02:50.522
Gradient descent then uses those gradients to update the weights in a direction intended to reduce the loss.

00:02:50.523 --> 00:02:54.205
The learning rate controls the size of each update.

00:02:54.206 --> 00:03:00.096
A very large update can overshoot, while a very small update may learn slowly.

00:03:00.097 --> 00:03:07.930
Training repeats this loop: make a prediction, calculate the loss, calculate gradients, and update the weights.

00:03:09.383 --> 00:03:14.215
Finally, we need to check whether the network learned a useful pattern.

00:03:14.216 --> 00:03:17.832
Training examples are used to adjust the weights.

00:03:17.833 --> 00:03:22.500
Evaluation examples are kept separate from those updates.

00:03:22.501 --> 00:03:29.175
If a network only memorises the training examples, it may perform poorly on new examples.

00:03:29.176 --> 00:03:36.795
Evaluation helps us check generalisation: how well the learned pattern works beyond the data used for training.

00:03:36.796 --> 00:03:46.303
To recap, a network transforms inputs into predictions, measures error with a loss function, and learns through repeated weight updates.

00:03:46.304 --> 00:03:49.256
Thank you for learning with CogniStream.

